  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   This approach uses a multilevel optimization strategy based on separated coordinates (MORs) and self-similarity detection to minimize the maximum correlation. The core idea is to represent the step function as a product of simpler functions on separated domains and optimize their parameters simultaneously. Unlike previous implementations, this method:\n   - Detects highly correlated shifts automatically\n   - Uses coordinate transformation to create self-similar structures\n   - Projects gradients onto feasible sets with variable scaling\n   - Maintains global optimum through logarithmic barrier functions\n   The main advantages are found in reduced local search space dimensions and preserved global optimum properties through barrier functions. The algorithm is designed to escape local minima by:\n     - Trying multiple coordinate decompositions\n     - Switching optimization modes between gradient-based and probabilistic\n     - Dynamically adjusting grid resolution based on local function properties\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   The optimization problem is defined as:\n     min h C_5(h) = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n     subject to:\n        0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n        \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n\n   The feasible set is approximated as {h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points | sum(h) = n_points/2}.\n\n   We reformulate using separation coordinates:\n     Let y = {y1, y2, ..., y_m} where:\n        y_j = \u220f_{i=1}^{k_j} x_{a_{ij}}\n   and use a three-stage optimization framework:\n     - Coordinate transformation with barrier function \u03c8(h)\n     - Direction-based gradient calculation\n     - Variable projection onto [0,1] and sum=1/2\n\n   The gradient is approximated using finite differences for selected shifts.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   Phase initialization:\n     h0 = (initial_h_values if provided else np.random.rand(n_points)/2)[0:n_small]\n     C_best = float('inf')\n\n   Main steps:\n     1. Grid transformation:\n        ds = 2.0 / n_points\n        x = np.arange(0, 2.0, ds)\n\n     2. CORRECTED Separated coordinates computation:\n        domains = [np.arange(0, n_small/16, 1) for _ in range(n_small)]\n        transforms = [generate_wavelet_transform(d) for d in domains]\n\n     3. Multi-level optimization loop:\n        while time_left():\n            candidate = optimize_multi_level(n_points, initial_h, C_best)\n            if evaluate_erdos_solution(h_opt)[0] < C_best:\n                C_best = evaluate_erdos_solution(h_opt)[0]\n                best_h = candidate\n            if improvement_threshold_met: break\n\n     4. Downselected solution (try resolution change first):\n        For resolution n, try transformations:\n            - Subtract/remove highly correlated components\n            - Add logarithmic smoothing\n            - Try hybrid optimization modes\n\n   Helper functions:\n     compute_optimal_transform(h)\n        - Computes self-similarity matrix S\n        - Identifies dominant frequencies\n        - Projects onto wavelet domain\n\n     reduce_correlation(h):\n        - Computes correlation thresholds\n        - Identifies shifts above threshold\n        - Performs gradient projection\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   Optimization parameters:\n     \u03bc = 0.1 (barrier parameter)\n     \u03c3 = 0.01 (inversion scaling)\n     perturbation_ratio = 0.3 (probabilistic step)\n\n   For grid selection, consider these variations:\n     Level 1 (fine): n_points=64\n        - Use probabilistic perturbation\n        - Identify 8 most correlated shifts\n\n     Level 2 (medium): n_points=128\n        - Active gradient projection\n        - Switch to logarithmic domain\n\n     Level 3 (coarse): n_points=256\n        - Use wavelet multiresolution\n        - Add probabilistic restarts\n\n   Hyperparameter schedules:\n     Inv_scale = max(0.1, min(0.9, \u03bc * 0.9999))\n\n   Local improvement trigger when:\n     max_iterations=2000\n     epsilon_convergence=1e-5\n\n5. Numerical robustness and memory use:\n   Memory management:\n     Use separable storage: X_cache = {}\n     For points with [0,1] scaling, use logarithmic storage to reduce projection failures\n\n   Projection algorithm:\n     def project_feasibility(h, sum_val):\n         h_proj = h.copy()\n         norm = np.linalg.norm(h_proj)\n         if norm == 0: \n             h_proj = np.ones_like(h) * (sum_val/(2*n_points))\n             return h_proj\n         h_proj = h_proj / norm * (sum_val / (np.sum(h_proj))...)\n         h_proj = np.clip(h_proj, 0.05, 0.95)  # avoid hard 0/1 binarity\n         return h_proj\n\n   Convergence handling:\n     Use logarithmic barrier scaling \u03b2=0.999\n     Implement fallback solutions based on separable coordinates\n\n6. Runtime safeguards and return contract:\n   Time monitoring:\n     break if (time.time() - start_time) > budget_s\n\n   Convergence:\n     Store best_ever = {'h': h_best, 'c5': C_best}\n\n   Termination conditions:\n     Max eval attempts: 1000\n     No improvement for 300 iterations\n     Grid feature saturation\n\n   Evaluation:\n     c5_value = np.max(compute_erdos_h_value(h))\n\n   Final return:\n     return(best_h_values, c5_value, n_points)",
